Description of the Vulkan ABI for AROS

1. Library

The library is named vulkan.library; its base variable is VulkanBase. The
initial version is 1. It is the Vulkan loader: it finds the drivers
(vulkan.hidd objects), owns the VkInstance, dispatches every command to the
driver the object it is called with belongs to, and implements the window
system integration (VK_KHR_surface, VK_AROS_surface, VK_KHR_swapchain)
itself, for every driver.

Programs link libvulkan.a (uselibs="vulkan", or -lvulkan), which holds the
stubs for the functions in the vector table and opens the library.

2. Vector table

The exact placement of each function is in vulkan.conf, which is generated
from the Vulkan registry by vulkan_gen.py and is committed:

  - the native calls (vkACreateSurface, vkAGetSurfaceAttr,
    vkCreateAROSSurfaceAROS) and a reserve for more;
  - Vulkan 1.0 to 1.4 core, in registry order;
  - VK_KHR_surface and VK_KHR_swapchain.

Functions are only ever appended. Commands of other extensions have no
vector: they are obtained with vkGetInstanceProcAddr or
vkGetDeviceProcAddr, as on every other platform.

All functions use the stack calling convention.

3. Library base

The library base is NOT passed to the library's functions: the pointers
vkGetInstanceProcAddr and vkGetDeviceProcAddr return are called as plain C
function pointers, and the functions in the vector table are the same
functions.

4. Function pointers

vkGetInstanceProcAddr returns the library's own entry point for a command;
it works with any instance, device or driver. vkGetDeviceProcAddr returns
the driver's function itself (or the library's, for the few commands it
implements or has to see), which is only valid for that device and its
queues and command buffers - the usual contract, and the fastest path.

5. Drivers

A driver is a subclass of hidd.vulkan (<hidd/vulkan_hidd.h>) that answers
moHidd_Vulkan_GetInstanceProcAddr like an ICD's vk_icdGetInstanceProcAddr:
asked for "vkGetInstanceProcAddr" with a NULL instance it returns that
entry point itself. The library calls no other method.

The first pointer-sized member of every dispatchable object a driver
creates (instance, physical device, device, queue, command buffer) belongs
to the library, as in the Khronos loader-ICD interface (VK_LOADER_DATA):
the driver must reserve it and not read it.

A driver's own window system extensions (anything with "surface",
"display", "swapchain" or "present" in its name) are neither listed to the
application nor enabled in the driver.

6. Surfaces

A surface is the inner area of an Intuition window, or a rectangle of it.
It is made with vkCreateAROSSurfaceAROS (instance extension
VK_AROS_surface, <vulkan/vulkan_aros.h>) or with the tag based
vkACreateSurface. The surface must be destroyed before its window is
closed.

7. Presentation

A swapchain is presented in one of two ways, chosen when it is created:

  - by scan-out: its images are the buffers of a scan-out bitmap of the
    window's display, imported by the driver (VK_KHR_external_memory_fd,
    VK_EXT_external_memory_dma_buf, VK_EXT_image_drm_format_modifier; the
    buffer's handle stands in the place of the file descriptor). The
    display shows the bitmap itself, through a hardware plane where it has
    one. Used when the device's driver is the one of the window's display,
    the format is B8G8R8A8, no more than three images are asked for and the
    display offers a plane for the size;
  - by read-back otherwise: the image is copied into a host visible buffer
    and written into the window's RastPort.

vkAGetSurfaceAttr(VKSA_PresentPath) tells which. The environment variable
VK_PRESENT=readback forces the second; VK_DEBUG (any value) makes the
library say on the debug output why a swapchain is not shown by scan-out.
VK_DRIVER=software or =all selects the software driver, or both.

A window closed with a surface still on it leaves the surface lost
(VK_ERROR_SURFACE_LOST_KHR); destroying the surface first is still the
rule.

VK_AROS_surface is not registered with Khronos yet: its extension number
and structure type value are provisional. Use the names from the header.
